---
/**
 * Diagram 1 — the stack, drawn as containment rather than flow.
 *
 * The concepts page argues that each layer *uses* the one inside it, so the
 * picture is four nested rectangles, not four boxes with arrows: an arrow
 * would imply a hand-off in one direction, which is the wrong mental model.
 *
 * Theming: every colour comes from a `--sl-color-*` custom property (mapped
 * from Quiet Utility in src/styles/custom.css), so the diagram follows the
 * light/dark toggle with no per-scheme rules of its own. No literal hex.
 *
 * Sizing: authored at 440 user units wide and capped at 27.5rem, so the SVG
 * never scales *up* past its authored size. At a 390px viewport the content
 * column is 358px, i.e. a 0.81 scale, which keeps the 14px layer names at
 * ~11px and the 12px role lines at ~10px on a phone.
 */
---

<figure class="qu-diagram">
	<svg
		viewBox="0 0 440 278"
		role="img"
		aria-labelledby="stack-title stack-desc"
		xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
	>
		<title id="stack-title">The vibe coding stack, drawn as four nested layers</title>
		<desc id="stack-desc">
			Four nested rounded rectangles. The outermost is Loops, which keeps the
			harness working until a goal is met. Inside it sits the Harness, which
			manages the agents and holds the context and tools. Inside that sits the
			Agent, which calls the model and runs the tools it asks for. Innermost,
			highlighted, sits the Model, which turns a prompt into text. Each layer
			uses the one nested inside it.
		</desc>

		<rect class="layer" x="6" y="6" width="428" height="266" rx="10" />
		<rect class="layer" x="28" y="52" width="384" height="198" rx="10" />
		<rect class="layer" x="50" y="98" width="340" height="130" rx="10" />
		<rect class="layer core" x="72" y="144" width="296" height="62" rx="10" />

		<text class="name" x="26" y="30">Loops</text>
		<text class="role" x="26" y="46">keeps the harness working until a goal is met</text>

		<text class="name" x="48" y="76">Harness</text>
		<text class="role" x="48" y="92">manages the agents, holds the context and tools</text>

		<text class="name" x="70" y="122">Agent</text>
		<text class="role" x="70" y="138">calls the model and runs the tools it asks for</text>

		<text class="name" x="92" y="168">Model</text>
		<text class="role" x="92" y="184">turns a prompt into text</text>
	</svg>
	<figcaption>
		Each layer uses the one inside it. The model sits at the center and does
		one thing; everything around it is scaffolding that decides what to send
		it and what to do with the answer.
	</figcaption>
</figure>

<style>
	.qu-diagram {
		margin: 2rem 0;
	}

	.qu-diagram svg {
		display: block;
		width: 100%;
		max-width: 27.5rem;
		height: auto;
		margin-inline: auto;
		font-family: var(--sl-font);
	}

	.qu-diagram figcaption {
		margin: 0.875rem auto 0;
		max-width: 27.5rem;
		text-align: center;
		font-size: 0.875rem;
		line-height: 1.5;
		color: var(--sl-color-gray-3);
	}

	/* Structure carried by hairlines, per Quiet Utility — no fills, no shadow. */
	.layer {
		fill: none;
		stroke: var(--sl-color-hairline);
		stroke-width: 1;
	}

	/*
	 * The single accented element in this diagram: the model at the core. It
	 * gets the same "recessed panel" surface tint the asides use, plus a jade
	 * hairline, and nothing else in the drawing competes with it.
	 */
	.core {
		fill: var(--sl-color-bg-sidebar);
		stroke: var(--sl-color-accent);
	}

	.name {
		fill: var(--sl-color-white);
		font-size: 14px;
		font-weight: 500;
	}

	.role {
		fill: var(--sl-color-gray-3);
		font-size: 12px;
	}
</style>
